General elections were held in [[Ghana]] on 7 December 2020. Incumbent President [[Nana Akufo-Addo]] of the [[New Patriotic Party]] (NPP) was re-elected in the first round after securing a majority of the votes.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2020-12-09/ghana-s-president-akufo-addo-wins-re-election-with-52-of-vote|title=Ghana's President Akufo-Addo Wins Second Term by Slim Margin|first1=Ekow|last1=Dontoh|first2=Moses Mozart|last2=Dzawu|publisher=Bloomberg News|date=8 December 2020|access-date=8 December 2020}}</ref> Former President [[John Dramani Mahama]] announced that he would contest the results.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kokutse |first1=Francis |title=Ghana's main opposition party will contest election results |url=https://apnews.com/article/ghana-elections-africa-john-dramani-mahama-west-africa-12cb47611276c43b5efc5b7a20218a1d |access-date=11 December 2020 |work=AP News|date=10 December 2020}}</ref> At the Supreme Court, a petition challenging the result was filed on 30 December, and unanimously dismissed on 4 March 2021 for lack of merit.

The NPP lost its majority in the [[Parliament of Ghana|parliament]], winning the same number of seats as the opposition NDC, resulting in a [[hung parliament]] with a single independent ([[Andrew Asiamah Amoako]]) in the position of [[kingmaker]],<ref>[https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2020-12-18/ghana-s-hung-parliament-sets-president-up-for-tough-second-term Ghana's Hung Parliament Sets President Up for Tough Term]</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Abdulai|first1=Abdul-Gafaru|last2=Sackeyfio|first2=Naaborle|date=2021|title=The uncertainties of Ghana's 2020 elections|url=https://academic.oup.com/afraf/advance-article/doi/10.1093/afraf/adaa028/6069415|journal=African Affairs|language=en|volume=|pages=|doi=10.1093/afraf/adaa028|via=|url-access=subscription}}</ref> who later chose to support the NPP.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.myjoyonline.com/npp-asks-speaker-bagbin-to-remain-impartial-as-it-maintains-majority/|title=NPP asks Speaker Bagbin to remain impartial as it maintains majority|website=Joy Online|date=21 January 2024}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
The [[President of Ghana]] is elected using the [[two-round system]], whilst the 275 [[Member of parliament|members]] of [[Parliament of Ghana|Parliament]] are elected in single-member constituencies using [[first-past-the-post voting]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Ghana: Vote Counting using Majoritatrian and First-Past-the-Post —|url=https://aceproject.org/ace-en/topics/vc/annex/vcy/vcy_gh|access-date=7 December 2020|website=aceproject.org}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.modernghana.com/news/984519/africa-watch-2020-is-election-season-across-afric.html |title=Africa Watch: 2020 Is Election Season Across Africa |date=13 February 2020 |work=Modern Ghana}}</ref>

Eligible voters must be Ghanaian citizens who are at least 18 years old, although those declared insane are disenfranchised. Parliamentary candidates must be Ghanaian citizens at least 21 years old, and either be resident in their constituency or have lived there for at least five of the ten years prior to the election.<ref>[http://www.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2123_B.htm Electoral system] IPU</ref>

==Campaign==
In February 2019, former president [[John Mahama]] was confirmed as the candidate of the opposition [[National Democratic Congress (Ghana)|National Democratic Congress]].<ref>[https://www.reuters.com/article/us-ghana-politics/ghanas-ex-president-mahama-nominated-to-contest-2020-election-idUSKCN1QG246 Ghana's ex-President Mahama nominated to contest 2020 election] Reuters, 27 February 2019</ref> In December, incumbent president [[Nana Akufo-Addo]] announced that he intended to run for re-election as the [[New Patriotic Party]] (NPP) candidate.<ref>[https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/Akufo-Addo-announces-intention-to-contest-2020-elections-822151 Akufo-Addo announces intention to contest 2020 elections] GhanaWeb News, 23 December 2019</ref> In June 2020, the NPP chose Akufo-Addo as its presidential candidate and Mahamudu Bawumia as its vice-presidential candidate. Mahama and Akufo-Addo previously ran against each other in both [[2012 Ghanaian general election|2012]] (with Mahama winning) and [[2016 Ghanaian general election|2016]] (with Akufo-Addo winning).<ref>{{cite web |title=Ghana's President Akufo-Addo chosen by ruling party for poll rematch against Mahama |url=https://news.yahoo.com/ghanas-president-akufo-addo-chosen-152620299.html |publisher=Yahoo! News |access-date=27 June 2020}}</ref>

All contesting political parties especially the NPP and NDC campaigned vigorously across the country despite fears that political activities such as rallies could increase the rate of spread of [[COVID-19]] during the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Ghana|pandemic in Ghana]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Fears Grow of Further COVID-19 Spikes Ahead of Ghana's Election |url=https://www.voanews.com/a/africa_fears-grow-further-covid-19-spikes-ahead-ghanas-election/6199020.html|access-date=7 December 2020|website=Voice of America|language=en}}</ref>{{Politics of Ghana}} Following the death of [[Jerry Rawlings]], first president of the Fourth Republic on 12 November 2020,<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Frimpong |first1=Enoch Darfah |title=Jerry Rawlings: Ghana's former president dies aged 73 |url=https://www.graphic.com.gh/news/general-news/ghana-s-former-president-j-j-rawlings-is-dead.html |location=Accra, Ghana|newspaper=[[Daily Graphic (Ghana)|Daily Graphic]]|access-date=25 November 2020 |date=12 November 2020}}</ref> most candidates decided to suspend their campaigns for a week out of respect to his memory.<ref name="bbc">{{cite web |title=Ghana's president declares seven days of mourning |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/topics/cnx753jejjlt |website=BBC News |publisher=[[BBC]] |access-date=25 November 2020 |date=12 November 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Korankye |first1=Kester Aburam |title=John Mahama suspends campaign in honour of Rawlings' death |url=https://www.graphic.com.gh/news/politics/john-mahama-suspends-campaign-in-honour-of-rawlings-death.html |newspaper=[[Daily Graphic (Ghana)|Daily Graphic]]|access-date=25 November 2020 |location=Accra |date=12 November 202}}</ref>

===Filing of nomination papers for presidential candidates===
At the close of the filing of nomination papers for the presidential election on 9 October 2020, seventeen persons had filed papers to stand for the elections.<ref name="nominations">{{cite web |title=17 Presidential aspirants to know their position by Wednesday, October 14 |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/17-Presidential-aspirants-to-know-their-position-by-Wednesday-October-14-1082368 |website=ghanaweb.com |publisher=GhanaWeb |access-date=16 October 2020 |date=12 October 2020}}</ref> One of those who filed their papers on 5 October 2020 was the incumbent president, Nana Akufo-Addo.<ref name="addofirst">{{cite web |title=Akufo-Addo files presidential nomination today |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/politics/Akufo-Addo-files-presidential-nomination-today-1078219 |website=ghanaweb.com |publisher=GhanaWeb |access-date=16 October 2020 |date=6 October 2020}}</ref> Each candidate was expected to pay a fee of GH¢100,000 to the Electoral Commission.<ref name="fees">{{cite web |title=Election 2020: Two teams to vet presidential candidates – EC |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/Election-2020-Two-teams-to-vet-presidential-candidates-EC-1079092 |website=ghanaweb.com |publisher=GhanaWeb |access-date=16 October 2020 |date=6 October 2020}}</ref> [[Akwasi Addai Odike]] of the [[United Progressive Party (Ghana)|United Progressive Party]] claimed that there was a conspiracy by the NPP and the Electoral Commission to prevent him from filing his nomination papers. This was promptly denied by the chairman of the NPP, [[Peter Mac Manu]].<ref>{{cite web |title=NPP, EC are ganging up to kick me out of 2020 presidential race – Odike |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/politics/NPP-EC-are-ganging-up-to-kick-me-out-of-2020-presidential-race-Odike-1080289 |website=ghanaweb.com |publisher=GhanaWeb |access-date=16 October 2020 |date=8 October 2020}}</ref> However, the NDC questioned why presidential candidates were filing nomination papers when the Electoral Commission has not finalised the new voter's register to be used for this election.<ref>{{cite web |title=How can aspirants file nomination without final register? – NDC quizzes EC |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/politics/How-can-aspirants-file-nomination-without-final-register-NDC-quizzes-EC-1077769 |website=ghanaweb.com |publisher=GhanaWeb |access-date=16 October 2020 |date=5 October 2020}}</ref> To the surprise of many{{who|date=November 2020}}, [[Jacob Osei Yeboah]] disclosed on 6 October, when he was expected to file his nomination papers, that he would instead be the running mate of [[Asiedu Walker]].<ref name="jacobyeboah">{{Cite news |last1=Frimpong |first1=Enoch Darfah |title=Jacob Osei Yeboah pulls Election 2020 surprise |url=https://www.graphic.com.gh/news/politics/jacob-osei-yeboah-pulls-election-2020-surprise.html |newspaper=[[Daily Graphic (Ghana)|Daily Graphic]]|access-date=20 October 2020 |location=Accra |date=6 October 2020}}</ref>

