===Final list of presidential candidates===
Following the vetting of their papers, the Electoral Commission announced the final list of presidential candidates on 19 October 2020. Five people had been disqualified from standing for the election as there were various problems with their nomination documents. These include IT issues with their forms and some had been forwarded to the [[Criminal Investigation Department]] of the [[Ghana Police Service]]. Those disqualified include [[Kofi Koranteng]] and  [[Marricke Kofi Gane]] who are both [[Independent politician|Independent]]. Akwasi Addae Odike of the United Progressive Party, [[Nana Agyenim Boateng]] of the [[United Front Party (Ghana)|United Front Party]] and Kwasi Busumbru of the [[People's Action Party (Ghana, 2018–2022)|People's Action Party]] were also not eligible.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Korankye |first1=Kester Aburam |title=EC axes 5, clears 12 presidential candidates to contest 2020 election |url=https://www.graphic.com.gh/news/politics/ec-axes-5-clears-12-presidential-candidates-to-contest-2020-election.html |location=Accra, Ghana|newspaper=[[Daily Graphic (Ghana)|Daily Graphic]]|access-date=20 October 2020 |date=19 October 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Election 2020: 12 out of 17 presidential aspirants cleared to contest |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/Election-2020-12-out-of-17-presidential-aspirants-cleared-to-contest-1088266 |website=ghanaweb.com |publisher=GhanaWeb |access-date=20 October 2020 |date=19 October 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Election 2020: Full list of presidential aspirants who qualify to contest |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/Election-2020-Full-list-of-presidential-aspirants-who-qualify-to-contest-1088332 |website=ghanaweb.com |publisher=GhanaWeb |access-date=20 October 2020 |date=19 October 2020}}</ref>
On 20 October 2020, the candidates balloted for the order in which they will appear on the ballot paper. The incumbent president, Nana Akufo-Addo will be the first on the list followed by John Mahama, a former president. The full list as on the ballot paper is below.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Frimpong |first1=Enoch Darfah |title=Akufo-Addo is number 1, Mahama number 2 on 2020 ballot paper |url=https://www.graphic.com.gh/news/politics/balloting-npp-picks-number-1-ndc-number-2-on-ballot-paper.html |location=Accra, Ghana|newspaper=[[Daily Graphic (Ghana)|Daily Graphic]]|access-date=20 October 2020 |date=20 October 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Here are the official ballot positions for presidential candidates |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/Here-are-the-official-ballot-positions-for-presidential-candidates-1089241 |website=ghanaweb.com |access-date=20 October 2020 |date=20 October 2020}}</ref>
Following this, one of the disqualified candidates, Nana Agyenim Boateng, leader of the UFP, took to court to seek redress and prevent the elections from going ahead but the Accra High Court found no grounds to grant this relief.<ref name="highcourt">{{Cite news |last1=Hawkson |first1=Emmanuel Ebo |title=Court throws out UFP leaders attempt to halt Dec 7 polls |url=https://www.graphic.com.gh/news/politics/court-throws-out-ufp-leaders-attempt-to-halt-dec-7-polls.html |newspaper=[[Daily Graphic (Ghana)|Daily Graphic]]|access-date=10 November 2020 |location=Accra |date=9 November 2020}}</ref>

