=== Violence ===
On 8 December, a day after the elections there were reports of gunshots leading to the arrest of [[Edwin Nii Lante Vanderpuye|Nii Lante Vanderpuye]] the incumbent Member of Parliament (MP) for the [[Odododiodio]] constituency.<ref>{{Cite web|date=8 December 2020|title=Odododiodoo MP Nii Lante Vanderpuye arrested|url=https://citinewsroom.com/2020/12/odododiodoo-mp-nii-lante-vanderpuye-arrested/|access-date=8 December 2020|website=Citinewsroom – Comprehensive News in Ghana|language=en-US}}</ref> The [[Ghana Police Service|Ghana Police]] reported 5 deaths and 19 injuries during and after the election.<ref>{{Cite web|title=5 dead, 19 injured in various electoral disturbances – Police|url=https://www.myjoyonline.com/news/national/5-dead-19-injured-in-various-electoral-disturbances-police/|access-date=9 December 2020|website=MyJoyOnline.com|language=en-US|archive-date=11 December 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201211162646/https://www.myjoyonline.com/news/national/5-dead-19-injured-in-various-electoral-disturbances-police/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Parliamentary election==
The Electoral Commission announced that following the creation of the Guan District in the [[Oti Region]] through the Guan District Local Government (Guan District Assembly) (Establishment) Instrument, 2020, people in this area will only be able to take part in the presidential election. This is because the Guan constituency had not yet been formally created.<ref>{{Cite news |title=No Parliamentary Election for voters in Guan District – EC |url=https://www.graphic.com.gh/news/politics/no-parliamentary-election-for-voters-in-guan-district-ec.html |location=Accra, Ghana|newspaper=[[Daily Graphic (Ghana)|Daily Graphic]]|access-date=7 December 2020 |language=en-gb |date=6 December 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=No Parliamentary elections at Guan Constituency – EC |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/politics/No-Parliamentary-elections-at-Guan-Constituency-EC-1127882 |website=ghanaweb.com |access-date=7 December 2020 |language=en |date=7 December 2020}}</ref>

==Opinion polls==
[[Ben Ephson]] of the ''Daily Dispatch'' newspaper conducted polls in the Greater Accra, Central and Western regions. He predicted that Akufo-Addo of the NPP will win the election with 52.6% of the votes while Mahama will obtain 45.7%.<ref name="ephson">{{cite web |title=Akufo-Addo could exceed our 52% projection – Ben Ephson |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/Akufo-Addo-could-exceed-our-52-projection-Ben-Ephson-1126490 |website=ghanaweb.com |access-date=7 December 2020 |language=en |date=4 December 2020}}</ref><ref name="joynews">{{cite web |title=Election 2020: Ben Ephson predicts 52.6% win for President Akufo-Addo (30-11-20) |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iA34dPTaJF0 |via=YouTube |publisher=Joy News |access-date=7 December 2020 |date=1 December 2020}}</ref> The Political Science Department of the [[University of Ghana]] polled 11,949 respondents and predicted that Akufo-Addo (NPP) will win 51.7% of the vote while Mahama wins 40.4%.<ref name="joynews"/><ref name="newsurvey"/> An iPoll survey of 670 respondents predicted 48.7% of the votes for Mahama and 46.7% for Akufo-Addo implying that the election may go a second round as the winner has to have over 50% of the votes.<ref name="ipoll">{{cite web |title='December 7 polls will head for a run-off' – Latest iPoll Survey predicts |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/December-7-polls-will-head-for-a-run-off-Latest-iPoll-Survey-predicts-1121906 |website=ghanaweb.com |access-date=7 December 2020 |language=en |date=30 November 2020}}</ref> The Governance Research Bureau predicted that Akufo-Addo will obtain 49.19% of the votes and Mahama 48.27% also implying a second round of elections may be needed.<ref name="newsurvey">{{cite web |title=Election 2020: New survey projects runoff |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/Election-2020-New-survey-projects-runoff-1121783 |website=ghanaweb.com |access-date=7 December 2020 |language=en |date=30 November 2020}}</ref>

==Results==
<!--[[File:Ghanaian Presidential Election, 2016 - Result by Constituencies.png|300px|thumb|Constituencies won by<br /><span style="color:#0070ff;">■</span> – Nana Akufo-Addo<br /><span style="color:#32cd32;">■</span> – John Dramani Mahama]]-->
The total number of registered voters was 17,027,655.<ref>{{cite web |title=Electoral Commission – Transparency, Fairness, Integrity |url=https://www.ec.gov.gh/ |website=Official website |publisher=Electoral Commission of Ghana |access-date=9 December 2020 |archive-date=9 December 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201209142740/https://www.ec.gov.gh/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> The Electoral Commission promised to declare the results of the election within 24 hours of the completion of voting.<ref name="24hours">{{cite web |title=Election 2020: I'll still declare result in 24 hours if I have it – EC Boss tells NDC |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/Election-2020-I-ll-still-declare-result-in-24-hours-if-I-have-it-EC-Boss-tells-NDC-1122344 |website=ghanaweb.com |access-date=7 December 2020 |language=en |date=30 November 2020}}</ref> Some leaders of the NDC expressed their doubts about the commission being able to do so within the 24 hours time frame.<ref>{{cite web |title=Don't hastily declare election results within 24 hours after voting – NDC warns EC |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/Don-t-hastily-declare-election-results-within-24-hours-after-voting-NDC-warns-EC-1118306 |website=ghanaweb.com |access-date=7 December 2020 |language=en |date=25 November 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=EC's promise to declare results in 24 hours described as a fallacy |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/EC-s-promise-to-declare-results-in-24-hours-described-as-a-fallacy-1116670 |website=ghanaweb.com |access-date=7 December 2020 |language=en |date=1 January 1970}}</ref> In the late afternoon of 8 December the Electoral commission communicated that the declaration of the votes in 24 hours  which was supposed to take place at 5:00&nbsp;pm which is exactly 24 hours of the closure of the election had been extended. The Commission in a statement explained that the extension was to ensure that collation of results at the constituency and regional collation centres across the country are accurate.<ref>{{Cite web|title=EC extends timeline to declare presidential results|url=https://www.myjoyonline.com/composition/comp-news/hp-news-3/ec-extends-timeline-to-declare-presidential-results/|access-date=8 December 2020|website=MyJoyOnline.com|language=en-US|archive-date=8 December 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201208163601/https://www.myjoyonline.com/composition/comp-news/hp-news-3/ec-extends-timeline-to-declare-presidential-results/|url-status=dead}}</ref> On 9 December, incumbent Ghana President Nana Akufo-Addo was declared the winner after securing a majority of the votes needed to avoid a runoff.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.africanews.com/2020/12/09/ghana-presidential-election-nana-akufo-addo-declared-winner-by-51-59/|title=Ghana presidential election: Nana Akufo-Addo declared winner by 51.59%|author=Redation, Africa News and Euro News|publisher=Africa News|date=9 December 2020|access-date=9 December 2020}}</ref>

===President===
The declared results exclude the figures for the [[Techiman South (Ghana parliament constituency)|Techiman South]] as the results there are being contested. The Electoral Commissioner states that the results of this constituency alone will not change the outcome of the election.<ref name="jeanmensa">{{cite web |title=FULL TEXT: Jean Mensa's declaration of 2020 presidential election results |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/FULL-TEXT-Jean-Mensa-s-declaration-of-2020-presidential-election-results-1129871 |website=ghanaweb.com |access-date=9 December 2020 |language=en |date=9 December 2020}}</ref> On 10 December 2020, the Electoral Commission issued a statement which revised figures for the presidential election results while admitting that there had been some errors.<ref>{{cite web |title=EC revises disparities in presidential election results |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/EC-revises-disparities-in-presidential-election-results-1130120 |website=ghanaweb.com |access-date=10 December 2020 |language=en |date=10 December 2020}}</ref> A research group Research and Grant Institute of Ghana raised concerns about this and  the [[Coalition of Domestic Election Observers]] (CODEO) urged the commission to come out and clarify the errors properly instead of just issuing press releases.<ref>{{cite web |title=EC's computational errors have tainted the credibility of 2020 polls - Research Group |url=https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/NewsArchive/EC-s-computational-errors-have-tainted-the-credibility-of-2020-polls-Research-Group-1130303 |website=ghanaweb.com |access-date=16 December 2020 |language=en |date=10 December 2020}}</ref><ref name="zurek">{{Cite news |last1=Zurek |first1=Kweku |title=EC must go beyond passive press releases to address errors - CODEO |url=https://www.graphic.com.gh/news/politics/ec-must-go-beyond-passive-press-releases-to-address-errors-codeo.html |location=Accra, Ghana|newspaper=[[Daily Graphic (Ghana)|Daily Graphic]]|access-date=16 December 2020 |language=en-gb |date=15 December 2020}}</ref>

